WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.269
Serving a Qwen

00:00:01.081 --> 00:00:03.475
model across 5,600

00:00:03.298 --> 00:00:04.725
AWS Lambdas

00:00:05.262 --> 00:00:06.535
Noah Lindner split

00:00:06.382 --> 00:00:08.516
a 9.3 GB Qwen3

00:00:08.328 --> 00:00:08.799
.

00:00:08.611 --> 00:00:10.615
5-4B fine-tune into

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:11.523
one Lambda job

00:00:11.335 --> 00:00:12.089
per layer per

00:00:11.901 --> 00:00:12.773
token block

00:00:12.998 --> 00:00:14.519
reading a 4,000-token

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.500
document took 17 seconds

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.563
and cost 3.4 cents

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:20.493
Under a 3 GB

00:00:20.305 --> 00:00:21.271
Lambda limit

00:00:21.543 --> 00:00:22.097
each of the

00:00:21.909 --> 00:00:23.677
model's 32 roughly

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:26.377
225 MB layers ran

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:27.427
as an idempotent

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:28.276
job that reads

00:00:28.088 --> 00:00:29.927
inputs from S3

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:30.705
and writes one

00:00:30.517 --> 00:00:31.578
object back

00:00:32.102 --> 00:00:32.998
Google sells a

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.224
hosted decision model

00:00:34.248 --> 00:00:35.592
Gemini Decision 1

00:00:35.746 --> 00:00:36.901
for $0.10  per

00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:37.727
million tokens

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.717
so the same

00:00:38.529 --> 00:00:41.146
read costs 0.04 cents

00:00:40.958 --> 00:00:41.465
there

00:00:41.982 --> 00:00:42.996
Full story on

00:00:42.807 --> 00:00:43.703
the dashboard
